                                                         <u> AB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  1

Date of Hearing:   February 10, 1999

                ASSEMBLY COMMITTEE ON EDUCATION 
                      Kerry Mazzoni, Chair
     AB 1 X1 (Villaraigosa) - As Introduced: January 19, 1999
 <u> 
SUBJECT </u> : California Mentor Peer Review and Assistance Program  
for Teachers.

 <u> SUMMARY </u> : Replaces the California Mentor Teacher Program with the  
California Mentor Peer Review and Assistance Program for  
Teachers.  Specifically, <u> this bill </u> : 

1)Sunsets the California Mentor Teacher Program by July 1, 2000  
  and establishes in its place the California Mentor Peer Review  
  and Assistance Program, which would become fully operational  
  on the same date.


2)Requires the Superintendent of Public Instruction (SPI) to  
  develop guidelines by September 30, 1999 for the  
  implementation of the Mentor Peer Review Program. The State  
  Board of Education must approve the guidelines by December 31,  
  1999. The guidelines approved by the State Board must provide  
  "maximum local flexibility" so that the governing board of a  
  school district and the exclusive representative of the  
  certificated employees in the school district can develop and  
  implement a program that meets local conditions.


3)Requires, at a minimum, the following principles to be  
  included in locally developed peer review programs:


   a)   Teacher participants in the program who have permanent  
     status and volunteer to participate or be referred for  
     participation in the program as a result of their biennial  
     evaluation. Results from the program must be shared with  
     the school principal for use in evaluating the teacher.


   b)   Written and clearly stated performance goals for  
     individual teachers that are aligned with pupil learning  
     goals.









                                                         <u> AB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  2


   c)   Review and assistance that includes multiple  
     observations of a teacher during periods of classroom  
     instruction.


   d)   Sufficient staff development activities to assist  
     teachers to improve their teaching skills and knowledge.


   e)   A monitoring component with a written record.


4)Requires a mentor teacher participating in this program to: a)  
  be a credentialed classroom teacher with permanent status; b)  
  have substantial recent experience in classroom instruction;  
  and c) have demonstrated exemplary teaching ability.  
  "Exemplary teaching ability" is indicated by effective  
  communication skills, subject matter knowledge, and mastery of  
  a range of teaching strategies.


5)Requires mentors to be selected by a joint teacher  
  administrator peer review panel that must also review peer  
  review reports, and make recommendations to the school  
  district governing board regarding program participants. This  
  duty would include forwarding to the governing board the names  
  of individuals who are not able to demonstrate satisfactory  
  improvement after getting help.


6)Specifies that a school district that accepts state funds for  
  this program agrees to negotiate the development and  
  implementation of the program with the exclusive  
  representative of the certificated employees in the school  
  district, if there is an exclusive representative of the  
  certificated employees.


7)Specifies that a school district that opts not to participate  
  in this program is not eligible for funding for mentor  
  teachers or the annual cost of living adjustment that is given  
  to school districts. Also requires districts not participating  
  in this program to report annually at a regularly scheduled  
  meeting of the school district governing board on the  








                                                         <u> AB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  3

  rationale for not participating in the program.


8)Authorizes a school district to notify the SPI that it plans  
  to implement a program and would require the SPI to apportion  
  funds to that school district for staff development activities  
  and training for district personnel that is necessary to  
  implement a program. Further specifies that state funding for  
  this program is subject to an appropriation in the annual  
  Budget Act.


9)Requires school district governing boards to evaluate and  
  assess certificated employee performance as it reasonably  
  relates to the progress of pupils toward the state-adopted  
  academic content standards as measured by state-adopted  
  criterion referenced assessments. Further authorizes a school  
  district to require that a certificated employee who receives  
  an unsatisfactory rating in this evaluation to participate in  
  its Mentor Peer Review and Assistance program.



 <u> EXISTING LAW </u> :

1)Establishes the California Mentor Teacher Program and provides  
  that the primary function of a mentor teacher is to provide  
  assistance and guidance to new teachers. Mentor teachers may  
  provide staff development for teachers and develop special  
  curriculum.


2)Requires the governing board of each school district to  
  evaluate and assess certificated employee performance as it  
  reasonably relates to the progress of pupils toward the  
  standards of expected pupil achievement established by the  
  governing board.



 <u> FISCAL EFFECT </u> :   This bill appropriates $100 million (Prop. 98)  
from the General Fund for the 1999-2000 fiscal year, with $16.8  
million for the Mentor Peer Review Program and $83.2 million for  
the existing California Mentor Program. The bill requires the  
SPI to apportion to participating school districts in the  








                                                         <u> AB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  4

1999-2000 fiscal year $1,000 for each mentor teacher funded by  
the state in 1999-2000. Districts may use this funding for staff  
development activities and training necessary to implement the  
Mentor Peer Review Program.

 <u> COMMENTS </u> :   This bill is one of four bills sponsored by the  
Governor for the special legislative session on education. The  
other bills are AB 2X (Mazzoni), which would establish the  
Elementary School Intensive Reading Program, SB 1X (Alpert),  
which would establish the Public School Performance  
Accountability Program, and SB 2X (O'Connell), which would  
create a high school exit examination.

 <u> Teacher Probation and Tenure </u> . Teachers who are new to a district  
are first put on probationary status and can be dismissed for  
unsatisfactory performance if they are notified by March 15.  
They can earn permanent status (also called tenure) after two  
years. The dismissal of teachers with permanent status is a  
formal process and is often very lengthy and costly.

 <u> Reimbursable State Mandate </u> ? This bill would deny cost of living  
adjustments to districts that do not participate, effectively  
making the program a statewide mandate. School district  
representatives have cited a state Supreme Court case (City of  
Sacramento et al., v. The State of California et al., 50 Cal. 3d  
51) that could mean that this mandate is reimbursable. School  
districts and teachers unions have indicated strong opposition  
to the denial of the cost of living adjustment for districts  
that do not participate in the program.

 <u> Can This Program Start By July 2000 </u> ? This bill requires that the  
Peer Review program become fully operational by July 1, 2000.  
Education groups have expressed concern that there would not be  
enough time for all of the state's 1,000 school districts to  
negotiate the details of local peer review programs by then.  
Staff recommends that the committee consider as an alternative a  
two or three year phase-in of this program to allow all  
districts sufficient time to prepare.
 <u> 
Use of Mentor Teacher Funds May Put BTSA At Risk. </u> Chapter 548,  
Statutes of 1998 (SB 2042, Alpert/Mazzoni) requires districts to  
put beginning teachers through an induction program such as the  
Beginning Teacher Support and Assessment (BTSA) program. BTSA  
requires districts to provide local matching funds of $2,000 for  
every participating teacher (the state provides $3,000 per  








                                                         <u>AB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  5

teacher); these funds may come from the Mentor Teacher program.  
A recent survey by the California Department of Education  
indicated that a substantial majority of the districts surveyed  
used Mentor Teacher funds for this purpose. By eliminating the  
Mentor Teacher program, this bill could put at risk those  
districts that use Mentor Teacher funds to match state funding  
for the BTSA program. 
<u> 
More Flexibility for Smaller Districts Needed </u> . Some  
districts-particularly smaller districts-could create stronger  
peer review programs if they could pool resources from multiple  
districts. Also, very small districts with few teachers that are  
unable to partner with neighboring districts should be allowed  
to not participate in the program.

 <u> No Evaluation Component </u> . This program does not include any  
provisions for determining its overall effectiveness. Staff  
recommends that the joint committees required by the bill  
evaluate the program and conduct exit interviews as part of the  
locally negotiated program.

 <u> Author's Amendments </u> . The author intends to introduce in  
committee amendments to this bill that would make several  
clarifying and technical changes. 

 <u> REGISTERED SUPPORT / OPPOSITION </u> :   (as of 5/5/99)

